**Problem.** For a commutative ring $R$, find the two-sided ideals of the ring $M_n(R)$ of $n\times n$ matrices over $R$.

**Answer.** The two-sided ideals of $M_n(R)$ are precisely the sets $M_n(I)=\{A\in M_n(R):$ every entry of $A$ lies in $I\}$, where $I$ ranges over all (two-sided) ideals of $R$.

---

**Proof.**

Since $R$ is commutative, every ideal of $R$ is two-sided. We prove two inclusions.

**Step 1. $M_n(I)$ is a two-sided ideal of $M_n(R)$ for every ideal $I\trianglelefteq R$.**

$I\trianglelefteq R$ is an additive subgroup, so $M_n(I)$ is an additive subgroup of $M_n(R)$. Let $A\in M_n(I)$ and $B\in M_n(R)$. The $(i,j)$-entry of $AB$ is $\sum_k a_{ik}b_{kj}$ with $a_{ik}\in I$ and $b_{kj}\in R$; since $I$ is an ideal, each $a_{ik}b_{kj}\in I$, hence $(AB)_{ij}\in I$. Thus $AB\in M_n(I)$. Similarly $(BA)_{ij}=\sum_k b_{ik}a_{kj}\in I$, so $BA\in M_n(I)$. Hence $M_n(I)$ is a two-sided ideal.

**Step 2. Every two-sided ideal $J$ of $M_n(R)$ equals $M_n(I)$ for some ideal $I\trianglelefteq R$.**

Let $J\trianglelefteq M_n(R)$ be a two-sided ideal. Define
$$I:=\{r\in R : r \text{ is an entry of some matrix in } J\}.$$
Equivalently, $I=\{r\in R: rE_{11}\in J\}$ where $E_{ij}$ denotes the matrix unit (1 in position $(i,j)$, 0 elsewhere). Indeed, if $A\in J$ has $a_{ij}=r$, then $E_{1i}AE_{j1}=a_{ij}E_{11}=rE_{11}\in J$ (using that $J$ is a two-sided ideal), so $rE_{11}\in J$ and $r\in I$. Conversely, if $rE_{11}\in J$, then $r$ is an entry of a matrix in $J$, so $r\in I$. Thus
$$I=\{r\in R:rE_{11}\in J\}.$$

*$I$ is an ideal of $R$.* If $r,s\in I$, then $rE_{11},sE_{11}\in J$, so $(r-s)E_{11}\in J$, giving $r-s\in I$. If $r\in I$ and $a\in R$, then $rE_{11}\in J$ and $aE_{11}\in M_n(R)$, so $(aE_{11})(rE_{11})=arE_{11}\in J$ (two-sided ideal), hence $ar\in I$. So $I\trianglelefteq R$.

*$J\subseteq M_n(I)$.* If $A=(a_{ij})\in J$, then for each $(i,j)$, $E_{1i}AE_{j1}=a_{ij}E_{11}\in J$, so $a_{ij}\in I$. Hence every entry of $A$ is in $I$, i.e. $A\in M_n(I)$.

*$M_n(I)\subseteq J$.* It suffices to show $rE_{ij}\in J$ for all $r\in I$ and all $i,j$, since these generate $M_n(I)$ as an additive group. If $r\in I$, then $rE_{11}\in J$. Using the matrix units $E_{i1}$ and $E_{1j}$ (which are in $M_n(R)$) and that $J$ is a two-sided ideal:
$$E_{i1}(rE_{11})E_{1j}=rE_{ij}\in J.$$
Hence $rE_{ij}\in J$ for all $r\in I$, $i,j$, and so $M_n(I)\subseteq J$.

Combining, $J=M_n(I)$.

**Step 3. The correspondence $I\mapsto M_n(I)$ is bijective.**

If $M_n(I)=M_n(I')$, then $r\in I\iff rE_{11}\in M_n(I)\iff rE_{11}\in M_n(I')\iff r\in I'$, so $I=I'$. Injectivity holds; surjectivity is Step 2.

---

**Conclusion.** The map $I\mapsto M_n(I)$ is a bijection between the set of (two-sided) ideals of $R$ and the set of two-sided ideals of $M_n(R)$. In particular, $M_n(R)$ is simple if and only if $R$ is a field (when $R$ is commutative), and more generally the ideal lattice of $M_n(R)$ is isomorphic to that of $R$.

